RELIGIONATLAS 1
map = England in 867
title = England in 867 - majority-population religion

# Scope: the prevailing religious affiliation of each hex's local population,
# not the faith of its ruler, an occupying force, or every individual resident.
# The single colour is a historical conclusion, not a request for visual diversity.
[identities]
# id | display name | colour | semantic scope
christian | Christianity | #7895B1 | Christianity as the prevailing religious affiliation of the local population; this umbrella includes varied Anglo-Saxon and Cornish churches and does not assert uniform belief or observance.

[regions]
# atlas region | identity | confidence | evidence and caveat
bernicia | christian | approximate | Northumbria had longstanding bishoprics and monasteries. Christianity is the local-majority reconstruction; survival of older practices and differences in observance cannot be quantified.
western-northumbria | christian | approximate | A Christian Northumbrian majority is the broad reconstruction despite uncertain political and cultural boundaries; uncertain ethnicity does not imply a pagan majority.
deira | christian | approximate | The Christian local majority is retained after the Danish victory. The Great Army is separate military context, and Halfdan's Northumbrian land division belongs to 876, not March 867.
lindsey | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
mercia-core | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
wreocensaete | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
