hwicce | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
middle-anglia | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
southern-mercia | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
middle-saxons | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
north-folk | christian | approximate | Edmund's kingdom had Christian royal and ecclesiastical institutions. Peace with and supply of the Great Army in 865-866 did not make the East Anglian population pagan.
south-folk | christian | approximate | Edmund's kingdom had Christian royal and ecclesiastical institutions. Peace with and supply of the Great Army in 865-866 did not make the East Anglian population pagan.
devon | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
dorset | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
somerset | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
hampshire | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
wight | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
wiltshire | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
berkshire | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
kent | christian | approximate | Christian institutions are directly visible in the contemporary 867 Canterbury charter S 338; the regional rule remains a broad population reconstruction, not a claim about every person.
surrey | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
sussex | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
essex | christian | approximate | Christianity was established across the Anglo-Saxon kingdoms; this broad regional rule does not assert universal personal observance or ecclesiastical uniformity.
cornwall | christian | approximate | The Cornish population and church were Christian, with their own institutional history. Kenstec's acknowledgment of Canterbury is datable only within 833-870; no c.930 St Germans settlement is backdated.

[units]
# Optional canonical unit overrides: aaa-bbb-ccc | identity | confidence | note
# None are used: evidence does not support finer majority-religion boundaries.

[military-context]
# Separate, non-colouring annotations: unit ID | group | affiliation | scope caveat
# This is not an identity definition and never enters labels() or legend().
088-000-050 | Great Heathen Army at Danish-held York | Predominantly pagan / non-Christian in contemporary Christian narratives | Military-group context only, not the majority religion of York or Deira; individual affiliations were neither counted nor necessarily static or homogeneous.
